When port isolation isn't possible (some PaaS environments only support one port), use **Spring Security** to restrict access at the application level. The key is creating a separate `SecurityFilterChain` that only applies to Actuator endpoints, leaving your application's main security configuration untouched:

```java
@Bean
@Order(1) // Higher priority than the main app security chain
public SecurityFilterChain actuatorSecurity(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http
      // This filter chain ONLY applies to /actuator/** endpoints
      .requestMatcher(EndpointRequest.toAnyEndpoint())
      .authorizeRequests()
        // /health and /info are public — K8s probes need them without auth
        .requestMatchers(EndpointRequest.to(
            HealthEndpoint.class, InfoEndpoint.class)).permitAll()
        // Everything else (/env, /heapdump, /loggers) requires ACTUATOR_ADMIN role
        .anyRequest().hasRole("ACTUATOR_ADMIN")
      .and()
      // Use HTTP Basic auth for simplicity — Prometheus can send basic auth headers
      .httpBasic();
    return http.build();
}
```

This pattern ensures Kubernetes health probes work without credentials, while sensitive endpoints like `/env` and `/heapdump` require authentication.
